, "prior_sd": 5.11, "poll_margin": null, "poll_sd": null, "poll_weight": 0.0, "fundamentals": {"margin": 12.19, "lean": 1.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 0.89}, "rating_margin": 12.5, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.7, "baseline_margin": 12.38, "analyst_adjustment": 0.62, "key_factors": ["Josh Riley is a well-funded incumbent with a 4-to-1 cash advantage and no outside opposition spending", "National Republicans withdrew support from Peter Oberacker, who trails badly in fundraising", "Prediction markets price Riley's win chances at 97-99%", "District is only slightly Democratic-leaning, but the 2026 environment favors Democrats"], "rationale": "Every expert rater places the race at Likely D or better, markets price Riley near certain, and Oberacker's abandoned national support and severe cash deficit leave him little path in a favorable Democratic environment. The fundamentals point to a double-digit Riley margin.", "watch": "Any late surge of outside Republican spending or a polling miss in a district decided by under three points the last two cycles would narrow the outlook.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Josh Riley (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Josh Riley seeks re-election in a Hudson Valley district that leans only slightly Democratic but has been decided by narrow margins in recent cycles. He enters November with a large financial edge, roughly $3.2 million on hand against Peter Oberacker's $400,000, and no serious outside spending against him.\n\nOberacker, a state senator, won the Republican primary but has struggled since: national Republicans withdrew their support in September, and he has drawn minimal outside help. Expert raters uniformly place the race at Likely D or safer, and prediction markets price Riley's chances at 97 percent or higher. The 2026 national environment favors Democrats, adding to the in